From 2130901d5a397331c7a224fb7053e7732d8f5467 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 01:05:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(items): preserve native item context in Java data

Retain the original Bedrock identifier after Java item translation so
client integrations can evaluate native item-name queries through
ViaProxy. Java mappings can rename legacy items and collapse custom items
to a shared Java type. Copy mapping-owned custom data before adding context.

Preserve compiled use durations from network item properties as ticks.
The target 1.26.51.1 server supplies 1,440,000 ticks for seven spear types
and 32 ticks for apple; authored use modifiers express the same durations
in seconds. Client animation consumers can retain elapsed use ticks while
using the native maximum. Leave absent legacy durations unspecified.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 query callback FUN_14221b950 resolves the equipped item's
raw-name hash from either the legacy item or its component document.
Native execution confirms those source branches. These fields supply
rendering context; inventory requests use the native inventory and
creative catalog rather than client-supplied context.

Carry compiled item tags, kinetic condition durations, and base swing
periods in immutable animation context. The native player scripts require
these fields to activate and time spear hold, use, and attack tracks.
Missing conditions stay zero; absent metadata retains native query defaults.
Native 1.26.51.1 callback execution verifies main-hand lookup, optional
condition flags, unsigned delay, signed condition durations, tag matching,
and swing tick conversion. Render metadata travels in standard custom
data so direct connections and ViaProxy share the same contract.

Capture immutable decoded stack context before Java rewriting. Carry the
native item ID, auxiliary value, block runtime ID, shield blocking ticks,
ordered restriction lists, and original typed user data in a versioned
viabedrock:item_stack custom-data compound. Preserve absent versus empty
user data and independent ownership through the Java NBT wire format.

Count remains the live Java stack count, and inventory network IDs stay
within inventory tracking. This context does not authorize requests or
substitute Java component equality for native item classification. Target
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 classifier inspection establishes that
these inputs affect native retained-hand decisions. Keep native classifier
and final visual verification gaps explicit.
